WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.950
Welcome to the Federal
Modernization Workbook's

2
00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:05.638
developer workbook,
Building Azure Security.

3
00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:09.576
This is Chapter 9: deploying
to test and production.

4
00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:12.223
The kit reads every week and writes once,

5
00:00:12.223 --> 00:00:15.913
so this runbook is about the
week, and about the one write.

6
00:00:15.913 --> 00:00:20.444
It takes the kit from the labs to a live
test subscription, then to production,

7
00:00:20.444 --> 00:00:24.112
with the same commands and one
settings file per environment.

8
00:00:24.112 --> 00:00:27.506
The chapter is a draft
awaiting the owner's review,

9
00:00:27.506 --> 00:00:31.479
and nothing in it has been run
against a live subscription;

10
00:00:31.479 --> 00:00:36.470
the output you'll see comes from
fixture mode, dated November 17, 2026.

11
00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.270
Why a runbook?

12
00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:45.460
Because the kit's weekly reads need
only Reader, but it makes one write:

13
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:49.356
the security initiative's assignment
at the intermediate root,

14
00:00:49.356 --> 00:00:51.708
in DoNotEnforce, from the guide's Step 9.

15
00:00:51.708 --> 00:00:55.812
That write is a plan, applied only
with the hash a reviewer approved,

16
00:00:55.812 --> 00:00:59.319
and its rollback is rehearsed
in test before production.

17
00:00:59.319 --> 00:01:03.757
The runbook has two stages: a live
test subscription, then production,

18
00:01:03.757 --> 00:01:07.882
with the same commands and each
environment's own settings file.

19
00:01:07.882 --> 00:01:12.994
Between them sits the go or no-go,
decided on the test stage's live evidence.

20
00:01:12.994 --> 00:01:16.490
None of it has been run against
a live subscription yet.

21
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.827
Before you start, finish Lab 2 of
Chapters 1 to 8 in the test subscription,

22
00:01:24.827 --> 00:01:28.715
and check every unverified call's
output against the portal.

23
00:01:28.715 --> 00:01:30.290
You need two identities.

24
00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:35.103
A reader for the weekly run, with Reader
on the intermediate root and the tenant

25
00:01:35.103 --> 00:01:40.065
root group, as a managed identity or a
federated workload identity, never a secret.

26
00:01:40.065 --> 00:01:41.629
And an operator for the plan,

27
00:01:41.629 --> 00:01:45.028
with Resource Policy Contributor
at the intermediate root,

28
00:01:45.028 --> 00:01:48.768
eligible through privileged
identity management and activated

29
00:01:48.768 --> 00:01:52.303
only for the change window, as
the guide's prerequisites set out.

30
00:01:52.303 --> 00:01:57.364
You need PowerShell 7 and the Azure
CLI on commercial Azure, output folders

31
00:01:57.364 --> 00:02:03.280
inside the boundary but outside any
repository, and a change record for each stage.

32
00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:10.197
Step 1 writes one settings
file per environment,

33
00:02:10.197 --> 00:02:12.730
from the kit's template, with no secrets.

34
00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.959
The command sets the environment,
the tenant, the intermediate root,

35
00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:20.736
the platform workspace, the hub,
the workload subscriptions,

36
00:02:20.736 --> 00:02:25.796
the required assignments, the security
initiative's name, the narrowest role its

37
00:02:25.796 --> 00:02:30.705
policies need and its enforce-by date,
the output folders and the change record.

38
00:02:30.705 --> 00:02:36.192
The loader refuses a placeholder left
unfilled, and any key that names a secret.

39
00:02:36.192 --> 00:02:42.200
Test and production each get their own
file, and both live outside any repository.

40
00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.863
Step 2 is the preflight, seven checks.

41
00:02:48.863 --> 00:02:53.300
The settings are complete, name
commercial Azure and have no placeholder.

42
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.000
No key names a secret.

43
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.639
The output root isn't inside
the kit's own repository,

44
00:02:58.639 --> 00:03:02.025
which fails, or any other
repository, which warns.

45
00:03:02.025 --> 00:03:06.440
With the live switch, the Azure CLI is
on commercial Azure and signed in to the

46
00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.032
right tenant, and Resource
Graph returns the

47
00:03:09.032 --> 00:03:11.625
subscriptions under the intermediate root.

48
00:03:11.625 --> 00:03:15.362
The unverified calls are allowed
for the stage, or listed.

49
00:03:15.362 --> 00:03:20.187
And in production only, the test stage's
evidence must pass its stage check.

50
00:03:20.187 --> 00:03:25.460
Any fail stops the stage, and the
result is recorded as evidence.

51
00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:30.260
Who's involved?

52
00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:34.948
The reader identity runs the weekly job
and the preflight, with Reader only.

53
00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:39.566
The operator applies the approved plan,
with Resource Policy Contributor activated

54
00:03:39.566 --> 00:03:44.185
through privileged identity management for
the change window, and nothing standing.

55
00:03:44.185 --> 00:03:47.279
The security officer and
the policy owner review

56
00:03:47.279 --> 00:03:50.297
the plan and its hash
before anything is applied.

57
00:03:50.297 --> 00:03:54.906
And at the go or no-go, the security
officer and the platform owner record

58
00:03:54.906 --> 00:04:00.040
the decision in the change record, with
the open findings and their owners.

59
00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.490
Stage 1 begins with the
first scheduled run.

60
00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:10.437
One command runs every area's
check into a dated run folder,

61
00:04:10.437 --> 00:04:15.453
computes the metrics, writes one
manifest, and records an evidence record.

62
00:04:15.453 --> 00:04:20.784
In fixture mode, on week one of the Contoso
estate, it found forty-eight findings,

63
00:04:20.784 --> 00:04:25.787
said this run is the baseline, and wrote
evidence record 1 covering the run,

64
00:04:25.787 --> 00:04:28.166
all nine area steps and the metrics.

65
00:04:28.166 --> 00:04:31.817
In the live test, read each
area's findings with its owner,

66
00:04:31.817 --> 00:04:35.865
and for every unverified call
compare one row with the portal,

67
00:04:35.865 --> 00:04:40.760
as each chapter's Lab 2 does, and
record the result in the change record.

68
00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:46.560
Step 4 plans the one write.

69
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.855
The script reads the assignments first,

70
00:04:48.855 --> 00:04:52.472
and plans nothing if the security
initiative is already there.

71
00:04:52.472 --> 00:04:56.268
Otherwise its plan holds the one
command from the guide's Step 9:

72
00:04:56.268 --> 00:05:00.574
assign the initiative at the intermediate
root in DoNotEnforce, with a

73
00:05:00.574 --> 00:05:03.275
system-assigned identity and the narrowest

74
00:05:03.275 --> 00:05:06.122
role its policies need,
instead of Contributor.

75
00:05:06.122 --> 00:05:07.678
It prints the plan's hash,

76
00:05:07.678 --> 00:05:11.410
and the security officer and
the policy owner review both.

77
00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:16.349
Step 5 applies it, with the role activated
for the window and the approved hash;

78
00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:21.210
a plan whose hash changed is refused,
and fixture mode refuses to apply at all.

79
00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:26.560
Step 6 is the smoke test: the policy
check shows the initiative is assigned.

80
00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.360
Learn says DoNotEnforce
evaluates without enforcing.

81
00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.935
Step 7 rehearses the rollback in test.

82
00:05:36.935 --> 00:05:40.970
Plan the removal, apply it with
its own hash, see the policy check

83
00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:45.548
notice the initiative is gone, then
plan and apply the assignment again.

84
00:05:45.548 --> 00:05:49.211
Resources a remediation
created stay until removed.

85
00:05:49.211 --> 00:05:53.711
In production the rollback is different:
once the initiative enforces,

86
00:05:53.711 --> 00:05:58.060
you set it back to DoNotEnforce, back
to audit, rather than removing it.

87
00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:02.285
In fixture mode, that plan printed
the update command and sent nothing.

88
00:06:02.285 --> 00:06:05.861
Step 8, a week later, is
the second scheduled run:

89
00:06:05.861 --> 00:06:08.685
it finds last week's run and compares.

90
00:06:08.685 --> 00:06:12.140
In fixture mode, thirteen
fixed and four new.

91
00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:18.102
Step 9 is the go or no-go.

92
00:06:18.102 --> 00:06:21.241
One command checks the test
stage's evidence folder.

93
00:06:21.241 --> 00:06:25.912
Go when the hashes match every file,
the chain of records is unbroken,

94
00:06:25.912 --> 00:06:29.751
and each step the test stage
needs, the preflight, the run,

95
00:06:29.751 --> 00:06:34.590
the nine area steps and the metrics,
has a passing record from a live run.

96
00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:39.044
Fixture records never count, so a
rehearsal can't promote anything:

97
00:06:39.044 --> 00:06:43.740
in fixture mode the folder's own checks
pass, and every stage step fails.

98
00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.956
Then the security officer and the
platform owner record the decision

99
00:06:47.956 --> 00:06:51.950
in the change record, with the
open findings and their owners.

100
00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:59.012
Stage 2 is production,
with the same commands.

101
00:06:59.012 --> 00:07:03.262
Write production's settings file,
pointing it at the test stage's evidence.

102
00:07:03.262 --> 00:07:08.187
Run the preflight live: its seventh check
fails unless that test evidence passes.

103
00:07:08.187 --> 00:07:09.936
In the change window, plan,

104
00:07:09.936 --> 00:07:14.099
have the reviewers approve the
hash, and apply only that plan.

105
00:07:14.099 --> 00:07:19.205
Schedule the weekly run under the reader
identity in the agency's own scheduler; the

106
00:07:19.205 --> 00:07:24.162
first production run is the baseline, and
every run after carries its comparison.

107
00:07:24.162 --> 00:07:28.165
Production's own evidence check
adds the plan and the comparison,

108
00:07:28.165 --> 00:07:30.537
so it passes from the second weekly run.

109
00:07:30.537 --> 00:07:33.211
Switching the initiative to enforcing,

110
00:07:33.211 --> 00:07:38.840
remediation and exemptions are the guide's
Step 9, each its own reviewed change.

111
00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:46.329
Each runbook step leaves a record
the security officer keeps,

112
00:07:46.329 --> 00:07:49.702
mapped to NIST's controls in
the kit's requirements file.

113
00:07:49.702 --> 00:07:53.814
The preflight supports least
privilege and configuration settings.

114
00:07:53.814 --> 00:07:58.240
The plan supports configuration
change control and impact analysis.

115
00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:02.090
The rollback supports change
control and the contingency plan.

116
00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:05.527
Each scheduled run supports
continuous monitoring,

117
00:08:05.527 --> 00:08:08.965
the component inventory and
each area's own controls.

118
00:08:08.965 --> 00:08:13.315
The comparison supports configuration
settings and continuous monitoring.

119
00:08:13.315 --> 00:08:15.905
And the chain of records
supports continuous

120
00:08:15.905 --> 00:08:18.903
monitoring and the protection
of audit information.

121
00:08:18.903 --> 00:08:22.815
The evidence supports those
controls; the assessor decides.

122
00:08:22.815 --> 00:08:27.378
The key security indicators are
FedRAMP's indicators for cloud providers,

123
00:08:27.378 --> 00:08:31.700
which the agency lines its own
evidence up with and doesn't report.

124
00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:37.713
What breaks, and what guards against it?

125
00:08:37.713 --> 00:08:41.644
A plan applied after it changed
would send a command nobody reviewed,

126
00:08:41.644 --> 00:08:44.749
so applying needs the approved
hash of the plan on disk.

127
00:08:44.749 --> 00:08:48.103
Lab evidence could start
production on fixture results,

128
00:08:48.103 --> 00:08:53.025
so fixture records never count, and the
production preflight checks the test stage.

129
00:08:53.025 --> 00:08:56.587
A standing write role would let
the reader change the estate,

130
00:08:56.587 --> 00:08:59.864
so reads need only Reader and
the plan's role is eligible

131
00:08:59.864 --> 00:09:03.213
through privileged identity
management, for the window.

132
00:09:03.213 --> 00:09:06.469
Output in a repository
would publish the register,

133
00:09:06.469 --> 00:09:10.812
so the preflight fails in the kit's
repository and warns in any other.

134
00:09:10.812 --> 00:09:15.855
And removal as production's rollback would
lose remediated resources and history,

135
00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:18.780
so production rolls back to audit instead.

136
00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:26.827
You can rehearse the runbook's
offline half on the fictional estate:

137
00:09:26.827 --> 00:09:31.662
copy the test settings, point the outputs
at a folder outside any repository,

138
00:09:31.662 --> 00:09:34.271
and run the preflight, two scheduled runs,

139
00:09:34.271 --> 00:09:37.417
the plan, the rollback plan
and the evidence check.

140
00:09:37.417 --> 00:09:39.442
The plan says there's nothing to do,

141
00:09:39.442 --> 00:09:42.768
because the fixture estate
already has the initiative.

142
00:09:42.768 --> 00:09:45.793
The runbook's scripts have
eleven tests of their own.

143
00:09:45.793 --> 00:09:48.450
And here's what done looks
like: the labs passed,

144
00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:51.388
one settings file per
environment with no secrets,

145
00:09:51.388 --> 00:09:54.466
the plan's hash approved
before anything was applied,

146
00:09:54.466 --> 00:09:58.663
the rollback rehearsed in test, the
test stage's evidence passing before

147
00:09:58.663 --> 00:10:03.330
production's preflight, and the weekly
run scheduled under the reader identity.
